{
  "rationale": "This is iteration 0, so there is no race telemetry or integration feedback yet. I am proposing a conservative baseline that a controller can be built on and that I can test against the first race. Five sensors spaced 15 mm apart across ±30 mm give a line-width-sized field of view. Sensors are placed 50 mm ahead of the axle so the robot sees the line before it reaches the wheels, which gives the controller lead time on bends. The 12 mm footprint is about half the 25 mm tape width, so a sensor reads a clear 0 or 1 over most of its travel across the line. Sensor weights are proportional to sideways position (-2, -1, 0, 1, 2), so the signal is roughly the lateral offset of the line in metres scaled by about 67. A 120 mm wheel track and 30 mm wheel radius give moderate turning authority without excessive yaw inertia. The 0.8 kg mass and 160 mm body length are mid-range. I expect this to complete the oval and s-bend at moderate speed. The main risks are a sluggish response on the slalom and a loss of the line on tight bends if the sensor lead is too short. I will adjust the sensor forward offset and spacing once the first race shows where the robot leaves the line.",
  "geometry": {
    "sensor_positions": [
      {"x": -0.03, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": -0.015, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": 0.0, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": 0.015, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": 0.03, "y": 0.05}
    ],
    "sensor_size": 0.012,
    "wheel_track": 0.12,
    "wheel_radius": 0.03,
    "body_width": 0.12,
    "body_length": 0.16,
    "mass": 0.8
  },
  "intent": "Build a symmetric five-sensor baseline with a forward sensor bar 50 mm ahead of the axle and sensor spacing of 15 mm. Sensor weights follow lateral position so the signal is proportional to line offset. Off-track margin is the 0.06 m floor, which the 12 cm body does not change. The expected result is completion on the oval and s-bend with moderate line error, with the slalom as the hardest case. Next I want to see whether the sensor lead is long enough and whether the 5-sensor spacing is sufficient on tight bends.",
  "feedback_request": {
    "measurements": ["completion", "progress", "completion_time", "rms_error", "max_error", "line_loss_events", "steering_oscillation", "noise_robustness"]
  },
  "learning": "No race evidence yet. Hypotheses to test: (1) a 50 mm forward sensor offset gives enough lead to avoid line loss on bends; (2) 15 mm sensor spacing with 12 mm footprints gives a smooth signal without dead zones; (3) a 0.8 kg mass and 120 mm track are sufficient for the 1.5 m/s average speed needed to finish in 3 s. Integrator feedback should report whether sensor saturation or line loss occurs on the slalom, and whether the robot's turning response is limited by wheel track or by mass."
}
